[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says one thing: the parser reads dates month first, 412 reports,
one fix queued. That is true of some of the reports. It is not true of all of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not one issue. It is two, merged because [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)
merges on subject line alone, and both read the same to a tired desk at 9am.
</Callout>

## What the table already shows

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

Two causes, computed from the export by the desk's own tool (`causes.ts`, `groupByCause`) — not
guessed, not reread by hand. That split is the real one. #4471 should never have been one ticket.

## The two problems

1. **The parser bug.** On `app`/`email` traffic, or `form` traffic through `booking-v2` (whose
   hint is correctly `DD/MM/YYYY`), the shared backend parser still reads the date as `MM/DD`
   regardless of locale, flipping it for every day-first tenant. **379 reports** (`parser`). →
   [issue #1](https://github.com/aealvarengal/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1)
2. **The hint bug.** On `form` traffic through `booking-v3`, the date field's own hint reads
   `MM/DD/YYYY` even for day-first-locale tenants, so people type month-first on purpose — and
   the form then reads what they typed the tenant's actual day-first way anyway. Same symptom as
   the parser bug, different cause, only on `booking-v3`. **33 reports** (`hint`). →
   [issue #2](https://github.com/aealvarengal/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2)

379 + 33 = 412.

The table's footnote — 5 reports "read as a one-day move" — is a cross-check the desk already
ran on part of the `parser` bucket, from `desk-notes.json`. It is not a third problem: those 5
stay inside the 379 above, exactly where the code files them.

## Why the desk didn't see it

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) is right that merging on subject is fast
and that splitting later is cheaper than two engineers chasing the same bug. It says nothing
about the other direction: two different bugs can also *share* a subject line, and merging them
hides that there were ever two. "Merge when unsure" needs a partner rule: **before you mark a
big ticket known, check whether every report in it actually shares a cause, not just a
subject.** `causes.ts` already has the logic to check this; #4471 just never ran it before
someone wrote the word "known" on the page.

## Lessons

- A ticket this size is not "mostly one bug reported many times" by default — it is one bug
  only if something actually checked.
- A footnote on a table is a finding somebody has to read and act on, not decoration. This one
  sat there since the table was built.
- The desk's own tool for telling causes apart (`causes.ts`) already existed. Nobody pointed it
  at #4471 until now.

